Building a targeted business contact list from OpenStreetMap is an effective approach, but you will want to expand beyond your initial list.OSM tags are structured as key=value pairs. In OSM syntax, w/shop or r/shop are not valid tag definitions. Instead, OSM categorizes physical businesses under a few top-level primary keys: shop=*, office=*, amenity=*, craft=*, and industrial=*.   Primary Keys to TargetTo catch almost every physical business or organization in a region, target these primary keys:1. office=* (Highest-Value Leads for IT Services)Offices represent companies with dedicated workstations, local networks, servers, printers, and employees. These are typically prime candidates for IT consulting or managed services.   Catch-all: office=* (matches any office type)Key specific values: office=company, office=lawyer, office=accountant, office=it, office=insurance, office=financial, office=estate_agent, office=architect, office=employment_agency, office=tax_advisor, office=telecommunication, office=advertising_agency.   2. amenity=* (Local Service Businesses & Healthcare)This covers institutions and public/private services that usually maintain localized IT infrastructure (EHR systems, POS terminals, client Wi-Fi, IP phones).Healthcare: amenity=clinic, amenity=doctors, amenity=dentist, amenity=pharmacy, amenity=veterinary, amenity=hospital.Financial & Professional: amenity=bank.Hospitality & Events: amenity=restaurant, amenity=cafe, amenity=bar, amenity=hotel, amenity=events_venue.Community/Education: amenity=school, amenity=kindergarten, amenity=college, amenity=library.3. craft=* (Local Trades & Contractors)Contractors, fabricators, and trade services often have physical shops or dispatch hubs with office staff.Catch-all: craft=*Key specific values: craft=electrician, craft=plumber, craft=hvac, craft=builder, craft=carpenter, craft=photographer, craft=caterer, craft=metal_construction.4. shop=* (Retail & Commercial Outlets)   Stores need reliable POS systems, payment processing, inventory tracking, and local networking.Catch-all: shop=*Key specific values: shop=car_repair, shop=computer, shop=electronics, shop=trade (contractor supply), shop=supermarket, shop=department_store, shop=furniture, shop=hardware.   5. industrial=* or landuse=industrialLight manufacturing, warehouses, machine shops, and distribution facilities often run specialized networks, legacy equipment, and administrative offices.industrial=factory, industrial=warehouse, industrial=machine_shop.Secondary Tags to Extract for Your Lead ListWhen you export or parse the data, ensure you extract the following metadata attributes attached to those nodes/ways:Business Identity: name, brand, operatorContact Details: phone, website, email, contact:phone, contact:website, contact:emailPhysical Location: addr:housenumber, addr:street, addr:city, addr:postcode, addr:unitExecuting the Tag Filtering with osmiumIf you use osmium tags-filter on your extracted PBF file, you can pass multi-key expressions. For example:Bashosmium tags-filter buffalo_clip.osm.pbf \
  nwr/office \
  nwr/craft \
  nwr/shop \
  nwr/amenity=bank,clinic,doctors,dentist,pharmacy,veterinary,hospital,restaurant,hotel \
  -o buffalo_businesses.osm.pbf
(Note: nwr/ tells osmium to filter Nodes, Ways, and Relations matching those key/value rules).
